import sys;sys.path.insert(0,'/home/claude/chb-pages/gen/rw/polish')
from _w import w
S=[
("Lee County housing at a glance","""<p>Along Alabama's eastern edge, next to Georgia, Lee County has Opelika as its seat and Auburn as its biggest city. The 2025 estimate is 189,881 residents, and a median age of 31.4 reflects the presence of Auburn University.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Housing measure (2020)</th><th>Lee County</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Households</td><td>68,728</td></tr><tr><td>Housing units</td><td>74,856</td></tr><tr><td>Vacant units</td><td>8.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Owner-occupied</td><td>58.1% of occupied units</td></tr><tr><td>Renter-occupied</td><td>41.9% of occupied units</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Nearly 42% of occupied homes are rented, so plenty of houses have had tenants. A buyer pricing a former student rental will focus on wear from years of use. Student leases roll over with the semester calendar, which forces a landlord to sell around tenants and explains the steady interest in fast closings and as-is deals.</p>"""),
("Which jurisdiction bills your house","""<p>The tax bill stacks county, city and school district levies, so where the house sits matters. An Auburn address and a rural address at the same price can cost quite different amounts each year to hold.</p><p>Lee County has four public school districts: Auburn City Schools, Lee County Schools, Opelika City Schools and Phenix City Public Schools. Cities are Auburn, Opelika, Phenix City (partly in Russell County) and Smiths Station. Towns are Loachapoka, Notasulga (partly in Macon County) and Waverly (partly in Chambers County). Unincorporated communities include Beauregard, Beans Mill, Chewacla, Gold Hill, Salem and Marvyn.</p>"""),
("Due dates and the Revenue Commissioner","""<p>Payment opens October 1, and the bill is late once December 31 has gone by. Then the state calendar takes over. January 1 marks delinquency, the file reaches probate court in February, advertising runs in April and the sale closes the cycle in May.</p><p>The Lee County Revenue Commissioner's office runs collection from offices in Opelika and Auburn. Checks should be written to the Lee County Revenue Commissioner. The offices do not process titles after 4 p.m.</p><p>The legal due date stays put even when a notice is late or never comes. Phone the office rather than waiting for the mail.</p>"""),
("Homestead exemption","""<p>To qualify for the state homestead exemption, the property must be a single-family home, your primary residence, owned and occupied by you on the tax year's first day, on no more than 160 acres. Filing is done at the county office.</p><ul><li>H-1 is for owners under 65 who are not disabled: $4,000 of assessed value off state tax and $2,000 off county tax.</li><li>H-2 fits owners 65 or older with adjusted gross income under $12,000, or permanently and totally disabled. State tax is exempt, and $5,000 of assessed value is exempt for county purposes.</li><li>H-3 covers owners 65 or older with net taxable income of $12,000 or less on a joint federal return, or those totally disabled, and exempts them from all ad valorem taxes.</li><li>H-4 is for owners 65 or older with income above $12,000: state tax is exempt and the regular county exemption still applies.</li></ul><p>Exemptions attach to the person living in the home. After a sale, the buyer must file their own, and so must any heir who keeps the house.</p>"""),
("How to appeal your assessment","""<p>If you believe the assessed value is too high, the county Board of Equalization is the first stop. Filing windows come from state law and track the announcement of new values, so ask the county for the current dates.</p><p>Once the board rules, section 40-3-25 of the Code of Alabama gives you 30 days from its final decision to appeal in circuit court. Serve both the board secretary and the circuit clerk and post a bond for costs. Ask for a jury within 10 days of filing the appeal.</p><p>Last year's taxes must have been paid before delinquency, or covered by a supersedeas bond for twice the tax. Without that the court must throw out the appeal, and it has no power to halt collection of tax already due.</p>"""),
("If the tax is not paid","""<p>Parcels that stay delinquent are offered at the yearly May tax sale. Redemption is still possible afterward: the owner, heirs, a mortgagee or another interested party has three years from the sale date, and a recorded lienholder is given extra time following written notice.</p><p>Under section 40-10-120, a minor or someone under a legal disability may redeem up to one year after the disability ends. A redemption does not by itself restore clean title, so a house with a tax sale in its recent past needs a careful title review before any closing.</p><p>Paying off a delinquent balance ahead of May sidesteps all of this. When the sale has already taken place, the redemption clock is ticking and the Revenue Commissioner supplies payoff numbers.</p>"""),
("Selling after a tornado or years of rental use","""<p>On March 3, 2019, a series of tornadoes hit Lee County and killed 23 people, with the worst damage in the Beauregard community southeast of the Auburn-Opelika area. Homes damaged or rebuilt after that storm, along with older rentals that went without repairs, are harder to sell through a standard listing.</p><ul><li>Storm-damaged houses raise repair and insurance questions for buyers.</li><li>Long-held student rentals can carry worn systems and tenants on staggered leases.</li><li>Inherited houses may have unpaid tax years and several heirs to coordinate.</li><li>Owners who live elsewhere struggle to oversee repairs and showings.</li></ul><p>Cash Home Buyers buys Lee County houses in whatever shape they are in. Send photos and an address; back taxes get paid out of the sale proceeds at the closing table.</p>"""),
("Questions to settle before you list","""<p>Three answers shape your timeline. First, is any tax owed for this year or earlier? Second, does a tax sale appear anywhere in the parcel history? Third, who has the legal authority to sign, especially for an inherited house?</p><p>Ask the Revenue Commissioner about the first two and a title company about the third. If the house has tenants, add a fourth: when does the lease end, and will the tenants allow showings? A clear set of answers lets a buyer give you a firm offer and a firm date.</p><p>Keep every document in one place: the deed, the tax receipts, any exemption filing and notes from your calls. If something needs correcting, the sooner you find it, the less it costs in time.</p>"""),
("What happens at closing","""<p>A title company runs a search on the record, finds anything owed and drafts the settlement statement. Tax still due is deducted from what you receive, and the Revenue Commissioner will quote a payoff when asked. With tenants in place, hand over the lease so the buyer can see when it ends.</p><p>Have your deed and latest tax receipt on hand. No lender is involved in a cash purchase, so no appraisal or repair haggling slows things down, and you largely pick the closing date. That is a real help when tenants, probate or a move are driving your schedule.</p>"""),
]
F=[("When are Lee County property taxes due?","Payment opens October 1, and December 31 is the last day before delinquency."),
("Who collects property tax in Lee County?","That is the job of the Lee County Revenue Commissioner, which keeps offices in both Opelika and Auburn."),
("When does Alabama hold its annual tax sale?","Delinquency starts January 1, advertising follows in April, and the sale itself comes in May."),
("How long can I redeem a house after a tax sale in Alabama?","In general you have three years from the sale date, per Code of Alabama section 40-10-120. Some lienholders and people under a legal disability get more time."),
("Do renters affect how I sell a Lee County house?","Often, yes. Close to 42% of occupied homes here are rented, so selling around a lease is routine, and as-is buyers can usually accommodate it.")]
w('lee-county-al-property-tax-guide',S,F)
